sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh prints a 16 to 17 line report, so a caller that needs only the level has to parse or count lines. A --quiet flag prints the single Recommended Level line and leaves the default report unchanged.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nA caller that wants one number from `recommend-level.sh` reads a report. `--loc 50 --files 2` prints 16 lines and `--loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural` prints 17, measured on this checkout. The level sits on one of those lines, so every caller either parses the report or counts lines. A `--quiet` flag prints only `Recommended Level: N (Name)`, and the default report stays byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` is a flag on the existing parser, not an environment variable. `--json` keeps precedence when a caller passes both flags.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: bash, already required by the script. No new package.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `worktrees/224-deepseek-final2` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a human report. Measured on this checkout, `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2` writes 16 lines and the full-flag example writes 17. The recommended level is the second line, behind a blank line. A caller that needs the level either parses text it does not want or pipes the report through `awk`/`sed`. The existing `--json` flag returns a whole object, so it needs a JSON parser and still hands back more than the caller asked for.\n\n### Purpose\nOne flag prints one line: `Recommended Level: N (Name)`. Callers get the answer without parsing the report, and the default report does not change.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add `--quiet` to the argument parser in `recommend-level.sh`.\n- Add a quiet renderer that prints only the `Recommended Level: N (Name)` line.\n- List `--quiet` in the `--help` output and the script header OPTIONS block.\n- Pin the default report and the quiet line with a regression test.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- The `--json` output shape. It already serves machine callers and stays untouched.\n- Scoring weights, level thresholds and phase scoring. The flag changes rendering only.\n- stderr messages and exit codes. They stay as they are so existing error handling keeps working.\n- A `-q` short alias. No caller asked for one, and the single-letter namespace is still open.\n- Other scripts that might call this one. A repository search found no shell consumer, only two Node test files.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Parse `--quiet`, add the quiet renderer, update help and the header |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/recommend-level-quiet.vitest.ts` | Create | Assert the one-line quiet output and the unchanged default report |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that matches `^Recommended Level: [0-3] \\([A-Za-z]+\\)$`, and the process exits 0. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the output before the change for every example printed by `--help`. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-003 | When a caller passes both `--json` and `--quiet`, the script prints JSON. JSON wins because it is already machine-readable. |\n| REQ-004 | `--help` lists `--quiet` in OPTIONS and shows one quiet example. |\n| REQ-005 | Error paths keep stdout empty, write the message to stderr and exit 1, with or without `--quiet`. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`.\n- **SC-002**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2 | wc -l` prints `16` and `recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural | wc -l` prints `17`, the same counts measured before the change.\n- **SC-003**: `recommend-level.sh --help | rg -c -- '--quiet'` prints `2`, one OPTIONS line and one example.\n- **SC-004**: `npx vitest run tests/recommend-level-quiet.vitest.ts` passes in the `runtime/cli` workspace.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` 3.2 or newer, already required by the script | The script does not run at all | No new dependency; the flag uses the same `case` parser as every other option |\n| Risk | An older installed copy of the script rejects `--quiet` with `Unknown option` | A caller fails loudly instead of silently getting the full report | Document the flag in the same release as the script change; the parser already exits 1 on unknown options |\n| Risk | A caller parses the quiet line and a later label change breaks it | Silent wrong answer downstream | Reuse the exact string `output_text` already prints and pin it in a test |\n| Risk | A future `-q` alias collides with another tool's convention | Rework of the alias | Ship no short alias now; add one only on request |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: The flag adds no measurable time. It skips formatting work the default path does, so the quiet run finishes within 5 ms of the default run on the same inputs.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: N/A. The flag changes stdout only. The script reads no credential, writes no file and makes no network call.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Output is deterministic. The same arguments produce the same bytes on every run.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` exits 1 and writes `ERROR: At least --loc or --files must be provided` to stderr. stdout stays empty.\n- Maximum length: The line is short at every level. `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` is the longest form, because the level name comes from a fixed set of four words.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A. The script calls no service.\n- Network timeout: N/A. The script makes no network call.\n- Invalid number: `--quiet --loc abc` exits 1 with `ERROR: --loc must be a positive integer` on stderr.\n- Unknown option: `--quiet --bogus` exits 1 with `ERROR: Unknown option: --bogus` on stderr.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A. The script is stateless and writes nothing.\n- Session expiry: N/A. There is no session.\n- Both flags: `--quiet --json` prints the JSON object. The quiet renderer never runs when JSON is requested.\n- Help wins: `--help` exits 0 from inside the parser, so `--quiet --help` prints the full help text.\n- No phase section: `--quiet --no-recommend-phases` still prints one line, because the quiet renderer emits no phase fields.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 3/25 | Files: 2 (1 script, 1 test), LOC: about 20, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 0/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N; the change is additive |\n| Research | 1/20 | Read one 780-line script and two existing test files |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 1/15 | Dependencies: 1 (bash) |\n| **Total** | **5/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe script scores this change itself: `recommend-level.sh --loc 20 --files 2` reports Level 0. The packet runs at Level 3 because the authoring task fixes the level, not because the change carries that risk. The extra sections are documentation overhead here.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | Default output drifts while adding the flag | H | L | Capture the pre-change output and diff it in the regression test |\n| R-002 | A caller passes both `--json` and `--quiet` and expects a line | M | L | JSON precedence is documented in help and pinned by a test |\n| R-003 | The quiet line diverges from the report line | M | L | Build both from the same `RECOMMENDED_LEVEL` and `LEVEL_NAME` variables |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One-line answer for scripts (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script that picks a documentation level, **I want** `--quiet` to print only the recommended-level line, **so that** I can read the level without parsing a report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Report readers keep their output (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** developer reading the report by hand, **I want** the default output to stay exactly as it is, **so that** the new flag costs me nothing.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does `--quiet` need a short `-q` alias? Not now. The plan ships the long form and leaves `-q` free.\n- Should `--quiet` suppress stderr warnings? No. Silence on stdout is the request; errors stay visible.\n- Does `--quiet` change the exit code? No. It stays 0 on success and 1 on input errors.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md` section `L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD`\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":65,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":59}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/224-deepseek-final2/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_f3592c9d0eac48f9ad5d5d5452369d6e","title":"Ran ls, echo, rg","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/ | head -50; echo \"===\"; rg -l \"recommend-level|RECOMMEND_SCRIPT\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/*.js","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/ | head -50; echo \"===\"; rg -l \"recommend-level|RECOMMEND_SCRIPT\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/*.js","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/224-deepseek-final2"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["ls","echo","rg"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
